INT. GRAIN ELEVATOR CONTROL ROOM - NIGHT

A cramped booth above a concrete silo. Dials, a swivel chair, a space heater. Through the window, conveyor belts hang motionless in the dark.

LEONIDAS PAPADAKIS (50s, county auditor, windbreaker) sits at the desk with a ledger open. He checks numbers with a pencil. He is calm.

Boots on the iron stairs. The door opens.

CORA BEAUMONT-TEAGUE (40s, elevator manager, rolled sleeves) enters with two chipped mugs of coffee.

CORA
Cream?

LEONIDAS
Black's fine.

She hands him a mug. He sips. She doesn't.

CORA
Long drive from the county seat.

LEONIDAS
Two hours. I like the quiet.

CORA
Nothing quieter than a silo at midnight.

He nods and turns a page. She studies the back of his head.

LEONIDAS
The weigh tickets for September match the bank deposits.

CORA
Good.

LEONIDAS
October's a little thin.

CORA
Rain. Hauling slowed.

LEONIDAS
Mm.

He turns another page. Behind him, a shadow crosses the window. Cora sees it. She doesn't blink.

LEONIDAS (CONT'D)
It's the volume I can't square. Four thousand bushels, gone on paper.

CORA
Spillage.

LEONIDAS
That's a lot of spillage.

CORA
It's a big elevator.

He closes the ledger. Looks at her for the first time.

LEONIDAS
I'd like to use your phone.

CORA
Line's down.

LEONIDAS
It was working an hour ago.

CORA
Storms.

There are no storms. Both of them know it.

He reaches for the receiver on the wall. She reaches for it too. Their hands land on it together. They freeze, her fingers over his, the heater ticking.

CORA (CONT'D)
Don't.

LEONIDAS
Let go.

She grips harder. He twists. The mugs rattle off the desk and shatter, coffee across the ledger. They stumble apart, both breathing hard.

LEONIDAS (CONT'D)
(softly)
I've already sent copies to the state office.

Cora lets go of the phone. She sinks into the swivel chair.

CORA
When?

LEONIDAS
Tuesday.

She begins, quietly, to laugh.

CORA
Then why did you drive out?

LEONIDAS
To give you a chance to explain.

Outside, the conveyor belts begin to turn on their own.
